body:
This bit is what I'm most worried about!

Having to do `.operator Ref<SceneTreeTimer>()` here is nasty, because we want `RequiredResult<T>` to transparently turn into `Ref<T>`.

If I remove this, I get (on gcc):

```
scene/main/viewport.cpp:2120:111: error: ambiguous overload for 'operator=' (operand types are 'Ref<SceneTreeTimer>' and 'RequiredResult<SceneTreeTimer>')
 2120 |                                                 gui.tooltip_timer = get_tree()->create_timer(gui.tooltip_delay);
      |                                                                                                               ^
In file included from ./core/io/resource_uid.h:33,
                 from ./core/io/resource.h:33,
                 from ./core/input/input_event.h:34,
                 from ./scene/main/node.h:33,
                 from scene/main/viewport.h:33,
                 from scene/main/viewport.cpp:31:
./core/object/ref_counted.h:131:14: note: candidate: 'void Ref<T>::operator=(const Ref<T>&) [with T = SceneTreeTimer]'
  131 |         void operator=(const Ref &p_from) {
      |              ^~~~~~~~
./core/object/ref_counted.h:135:14: note: candidate: 'void Ref<T>::operator=(Ref<T>&&) [with T = SceneTreeTimer]'
  135 |         void operator=(Ref &&p_from) {
      |              ^~~~~~~~
./core/object/ref_counted.h:153:14: note: candidate: 'void Ref<T>::operator=(const Variant&) [with T = SceneTreeTimer]'
  153 |         void operator=(const Variant &p_variant) {
      |              ^~~~~~~~
```

It seems the problem is the that `RequiredResult` also has `operator Variant()`, so there's two paths to get here. It would be great if the compiler selected the shorter path (which is just to go straight to `Ref<T>`) but it seems to consider them to be on the same level

